          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What were the verified findings of the Czech finswimming double-blind trial?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In the randomized, double-blind, crossover trial conducted by Palacký University Olomouc (NCT05799911), 0.9 ppm hydrogen-rich water supplementation resulted in a statistically significant +0.6% improvement in 400 m finswimming time (P=0.003), crossing the smallest worthwhile change (SWC=0.4%) threshold. However, Cohen's d effect size was -0.07 (trivial), and four other endpoints (50 m sprint, blood lactate, RPE, and plasma protein carbonyls) showed zero significant difference (P>=0.12), with 4 out of 12 athletes exhibiting zero response. The complete dataset is openly hosted on Zenodo."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does UK Case A (NCT06788015) compare with Czech Case B in scientific transparency?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both studies utilized high-standard randomized double-blind crossover protocols. The critical divergence lies in disclosure: UK brand HtoEAU claimed on its website that four athletic and wellbeing metrics improved significantly, yet no outcome data were posted to ClinicalTrials.gov and no peer-reviewed paper exists. In contrast, the Czech study published two peer-reviewed journal papers (IJSPP and Frontiers in Physiology) fully detailing null findings alongside the 0.6% shift, and deposited raw anonymized data on Zenodo."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is +0.6% characterized as 'modest yet meaningful' in sports physiology?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The study utilized two distinct benchmarks: in competitive sports science, the smallest worthwhile change (SWC) of 0.4% represents practical significance, and 0.6% is 1.5 times the SWC. However, the standardized effect size Cohen's d was -0.07, categorized as trivial relative to overall cohort variation. Statistical significance (P=0.003) confirmed that the shift was not random error under crossover controls, yet the absolute physical effect was minute and blood lactate remained unchanged."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
