        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is hydrogen agriculture — and is it the same thing as hydrogen energy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Hydrogen agriculture refers to hydrogen or hydrogen-bearing materials acting on crops and water, originating from the three-stage spillover of hydrogen medicine into hydrogen biology and then agriculture. Hydrogen energy (energy hydrogen) addresses decarbonisation of fertiliser, farm machinery fuel and cold chains. The two differ in customers, price points and validation methods, and confusing them is the industry's most common framing error."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is there real scientific evidence behind hydrogen agriculture?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes — and plenty of it. More than 1,500 papers have been published worldwide, roughly 90% of them from China. Hydrogen has been listed as the fourth gas-signalling molecule since 2011, with mechanistic work on stress tolerance, heavy-metal immobilisation, saline-alkali adaptation and post-harvest preservation (see Science & Technology Review 2024, No. 15, and Wang et al. 2020)."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "If there are so many papers, why no large-scale adoption yet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Because papers prove an effect, not a deliverable. Between paper and industry sit six things that must be built separately: metrology and standards, product formulation, field reproducibility, cost ledgers, channels and service, and testing plus insurance. The industry still lacks a unified measurement caliber — and any reproducible, independently accounted case."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I choose between the device route and the material route?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Choose by scenario, not by technology. Powered, piped, high-value protected agriculture and turf can justify electrolysis devices; unpowered, dispersed, low-cost saline-alkali fields and open farmland call for solid materials. Fertiliser substitution and machinery fuel belong to the energy route, which decarbonises inputs rather than agronomy. There is no best technology — only scenario fit."
          }
        },
